        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Samarkand</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">c. 700 BCE – present</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Founded</span><span class="info-stat-val">The date of founding is uncertain; researchers at the Institute of Archaeology of Samarkand date it to around 700 BCE, and it has been proposed that the city was established in the 8th or 7th century BCE.</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Where</span><span class="info-stat-val">In the valley of the Zarafshan river in south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbekistan">Uzbekistan</a>, 270 km by road from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tashkent">Tashkent</a> and 240 km from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukhara">Bukhara</a>.</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Known for</span><span class="info-stat-val">The Sogdian city of Marakanda; conquest by Alexander in 329 BCE; the legend of the first paper mill of the Islamic world; the Mongol sack of 1220; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a>’s capital; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulugh-beg">Ulugh Beg</a>’s observatory; the Registan, the Gur-i Amir and Shah-i-Zinda</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Today</span><span class="info-stat-val">Samarkand is the second-largest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbekistan">Uzbekistan</a> and the capital of the Samarkand Region.</span></div>
