    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Samarkand was a central city of the Sogdian civilisation and was conquered in turn by Alexander in 329 BCE, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanians</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyads</a> in about 712, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> in 1220 and the Russians in 1868.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a> made it his capital in 1370 and rebuilt most of it, and his grandson <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulugh-beg">Ulugh Beg</a> built a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrasa">madrasa</a> on the Registan and an observatory there in 1428.</li><li>Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbeks">Uzbek</a> khans, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> the city kept its monuments, though some were destroyed, and Persian remained its literary language.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>According to legend, the secret of papermaking was obtained from two Chinese prisoners taken at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-talas">Battle of Talas</a> in 751, which led to the first paper mill in the Islamic world, at Samarkand.</p></aside>
    </section>

