    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">c. 700 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">Researchers date the founding of the city to about this time</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">329 BCE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> conquers Marakanda</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 712</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qutayba-ibn-muslim">Qutayba ibn Muslim</a> captures the city for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyad Caliphate</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1220</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a> take and plunder the city</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1370</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a> makes Samarkand his capital</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1428</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulugh-beg">Ulugh Beg</a> builds his observatory</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1868</span><span class="rf-text">Russian forces under General von Kaufman take the citadel</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1925</span><span class="rf-text">Samarkand becomes the first capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbeks">Uzbek</a> SSR</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/afrasiab"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/afrasiab.jpg" alt="Afrasiab" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/afrasiab">Afrasiab</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Afrasiab_-_details_from_The_Ambassadors'_Painting.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Samarkand stands in the valley of the Zarafshan river in south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbekistan">Uzbekistan</a>. Excavations within the city and in its suburbs have unearthed evidence of human activity 40,000 years old, from the Upper Palaeolithic, and a group of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesolithic">Mesolithic</a> sites lies in the suburbs. There is no direct evidence of when the city was founded. Researchers at the Institute of Archaeology of Samarkand date it to around 700 BCE, and it has been proposed that it was established between the 8th and 7th centuries BCE. The canals that brought water to the city and its suburbs, the Syob and the Darg’om, appeared around the 7th to 5th centuries BCE.</p><p>After the Indo-Iranian migrations of the second millennium BCE Samarkand became one of the main centres of the Sogdian civilisation, and under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid dynasty</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> it was the capital of the Sogdian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satrapy">satrapy</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> called it Marakanda. Some sources derive the present name from the Sogdian samar (stone) and kand (fort or town), which cannot be proved; the 11th-century scholar Mahmud al-Kashghari gave the Karakhanid Turkic form Sämizkänd, “fat city”. Archaeologists have found remains of Nestorian buildings at the ancient site of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afrasiab">Afrasiyab</a>. In the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> the city had four rows of defensive walls and four gates.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="growth">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Growth &amp; Great Ages</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/madrasa"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/madrasa.jpg" alt="Madrasa" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/madrasa">Madrasa</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:RegistanSquare_Samarkand.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Gustavo Jeronimo from Aranjuez, Spain; derivative work by MrPanyGoff, CC BY 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Alexander took the city in 329 BCE. It suffered significant damage but recovered rapidly under Hellenic influence, which brought new construction techniques: square bricks replaced oblong ones, and better masonry and plastering appeared. It then passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucid Empire</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greco-bactrian-kingdom">Greco-Bactrian Kingdom</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kushan-empire">Kushan Empire</a>. When the Kushans lost Sogdia in the 3rd century CE Samarkand declined as a centre of power, and it did not revive significantly until the 5th century.</p><p>Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samanid-empire">Samanids</a> (875 to 999) the city was a capital and a node of trade routes, and New Persian gradually replaced Sogdian as the main language of the region. From 1040 to 1212 it was the capital of the Western Qarakhanid Khaganate, whose khan Ibrahim Tamgach (1040 to 1068) built a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrasa">madrasa</a> at state expense and had a public hospital and a madrasa where medicine was also taught established.</p><p>The city’s greatest period came under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a>, who made it his capital in 1370 and over 35 years rebuilt most of it, settling in it artisans and craftsmen from across his empire. By his orders walls 8 km in circumference separated the city from its surroundings. The city then had a population of about 150,000. Ruy Gonzalez de Clavijo, the ambassador of Henry III of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a>, visited between 8 September and 20 November 1404 and recorded the building that never stopped. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulugh-beg">Ulugh Beg</a> continued the work from 1409.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="rulers">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Who Ruled It</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-shaybani"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/muhammad-shaybani.jpg" alt="Muhammad Shaybani" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-shaybani">Muhammad Shaybani</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Muhammad_Shaybani.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Kamāl ud-Dīn Behzād, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Samarkand was a Sogdian city under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenids</a> and fell to Alexander in 329 BCE. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucids</a>, Greco-Bactrians and Kushans, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanians</a> took it around 260 CE. The Xionites conquered it between 350 and 375 CE, it was part of the Kidarite state from 457 to 509, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hephthalites">Hephthalites</a> held it until the Gokturks, allied with the Sasanians, won it around 560.</p><p>The armies of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyad Caliphate</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qutayba-ibn-muslim">Qutayba ibn Muslim</a> captured the city around 712. The Abbasid commander <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-muslim">Abu Muslim</a>, governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khorasan">Khorasan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transoxiana">Transoxiana</a> from 750 to 755, made it his residence. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samanid-empire">Samanids</a> ruled from 875 to 999, followed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kara-khanid-khanate">Karakhanids</a>, the Seljuqs and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khwarazmshah">Khwarazmshahs</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> took the city in 1220, and it remained part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chagatai-khanate">Chagatai Khanate</a> until 1370, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a> made it the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timurid-empire">Timurid Empire</a>.</p><p>Nomadic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbeks">Uzbek</a> warriors took control in 1500, and in 1501 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-shaybani">Muhammad Shaybani</a> took the city and was crowned there, making it the capital of the new Bukhara Khanate. From 1599 to 1756 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/janid-dynasty">Ashtarkhanids</a> ruled it as part of that khanate, and from 1756 to 1868 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manghud">Manghud</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">emirs</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukhara">Bukhara</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> annexed the city in 1868, and in 1886 it became the capital of the Samarkand Oblast of Russian Turkestan.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="life">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Life in the City</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/manichaeism"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/manichaeism.jpg" alt="Manichaeism" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/manichaeism">Manichaeism</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Manicheans.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Samarkand was long a place of many faiths. Under Sasanian rule it was an important site of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manichaeism">Manichaeism</a>. A Nestorian chair was established in the 5th century and became a metropolitanate at the start of the 8th, and when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyads</a> captured the city most of its people were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zoroastrianism">Zoroastrians</a>, with Buddhists, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindus</a>, Manichaeans, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> and Christians among them. Qutayba settled an Arab garrison in the city, razed its Zoroastrian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fire-temple">fire temples</a> and had a mosque built, and most of the population adopted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benjamin-of-tudela">Benjamin of Tudela</a> reported 50,000 Jews in the city in 1170. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> the khanate allowed Christian bishoprics, and a Roman Catholic eparchy served several thousand Catholics from 1329 to 1359.</p><p>The city also became a centre of learning. According to legend, the first paper mill of the Islamic world was founded there after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-talas">Battle of Talas</a> in 751. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulugh-beg">Ulugh Beg</a> astronomers and mathematicians gathered from across the Islamic world, among them Jamshid al-Kashi, Qadi Zada al-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rumi">Rumi</a> and Ali Qushji, and in 1428 he built an observatory whose chief instrument was a wall quadrant with a radius of 40 metres. Many classical Persian poets lived in or visited Samarkand, and Tajik, a variety of Persian, is still reported as the most common native language of the city, though no <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> was taken in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbekistan">Uzbekistan</a> between 1989 and 2026.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="crises">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Sieges &amp; Disasters</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/mongols"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mongols.jpg" alt="Mongols" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bataille_entre_mongols_%26_chinois_(1211).jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Sayf al-Vâhidî. Hérât. Afghanistan, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> took Samarkand in 1220. According to Juvayni, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a> killed all who had taken refuge in the citadel and the mosque, pillaged the city completely and conscripted 30,000 young men and 30,000 craftsmen. The conquest destroyed most of the city’s monuments, though the complex of Qutham ibn Abbas survived. Khan Baraq sacked the city again to pay an army. In 1365 a revolt against Chagatai rule, the Sarbadar uprising, centred on the Registan.</p><p>The city was largely abandoned in the early 1720s, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nader-shah">Nader Shah</a> invaded the region in 1740. In 1868 a Russian force under General von Kaufman took the citadel, and the small Russian garrison of 500 men was then besieged; an assault led by Abdul Malik Tura, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baba-beg">Baba Beg</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shahrisabz">Shahrisabz</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jura-beg">Jura Beg</a> of Kitab was repelled with heavy losses. An earthquake in 1897 destroyed the marble columns of the Bibi-Khanym mosque.</p><p>The Soviet period destroyed some of the city’s buildings. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrasa">madrasa</a> of Shaybani Khan was completely destroyed, and most of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> churches built in the early 20th century were demolished. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> a number of Samarkand’s citizens were sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smolensk">Smolensk</a>, and many were captured or killed there, while thousands of refugees from the occupied western regions of the USSR fled to the city.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/ulugh-beg"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ulugh-beg.jpg" alt="Ulugh Beg" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/ulugh-beg">Ulugh Beg</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ulugh_Beg,_Timurid_painting_1425-50.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown artist, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Timurid and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbeks">Uzbek</a> periods made the most important architectural contributions to the city: the Registan, whose first building was the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrasa">madrasa</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulugh-beg">Ulugh Beg</a> built between 1417 and 1420, with the Sherdar and Tilakari madrasas added between 1612 and 1656; the Gur-i Amir mausoleum; the Bibi-Khanym mosque, begun by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a> after his campaign in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> of 1398 and 1399, which had about 450 marble columns raised with the help of 95 elephants; and the Shah-i-Zinda necropolis, founded in the 11th century by the Qarakhanid rulers. Their cobalt and turquoise tilework is well known.</p><p>The Russian section of the city was built west of the old city after 1868, and the Trans-Caspian railway reached Samarkand in 1888. It was the capital of the Uzbek SSR from 1925 to 1930, before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tashkent">Tashkent</a> replaced it. The city’s 2,500th anniversary was widely celebrated in 1970. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> added Samarkand to its World Heritage List in 2001 as Samarkand, Crossroads of Cultures.</p><p>The city is today the second largest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbekistan">Uzbekistan</a>, with about 882,000 people. It is divided into an old city with the historical monuments and a new city developed under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>. Its people include <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tajiks">Tajiks</a>, Uzbeks, Russians and many smaller communities, and the ethnic balance is disputed.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Samarkand, in the Zarafshan valley of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbekistan">Uzbekistan</a>, was a Sogdian city taken by Alexander, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> and the Russians. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a> made it his capital in 1370, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulugh-beg">Ulugh Beg</a> built his observatory there. It is the second-largest city of Uzbekistan.</em></p>

